  "notes": "The Daptone house band with its own frontwoman — the rhythm section that supplied *Back to Black*. The entry describes a recording method, and every drum number here is that method's consequence: \"one room mic and a kick mic, heavy tea-towel damping, no reverb, snare tuned high and hit hard on 2&4, hats closed and quiet, and no 16th subdivision carpet. Fills are single-stroke march figures. Recorded to 8-track tape, live in one room, so the kit's bleed is part of the timbre.\" That gives the loudest accent tier in the section against the quietest hat velocities, a fill density of 0.1, straight-16th single-stroke rolls with almost no gap or descent, and a `quantizeStrength` of 0.62 because there is no click. The band is 90–128 with the entry's slow-burn subset at 62–78, so the range spans both. Harmony is \"riff-derived, with the horn line supplying the changes\" — \"minor-mode funk vamps (2-bar, dorian, m7) for the up-tempos and major-key `I–IV–V` gospel-soul for the ballads\" — so the single-chord modal vamp carries the top weight and I–IV–V the second, with no modulation. The horn section \"answers every line\", which is the densest countermelody of any artist model in this section. Bass is \"flatwound electric through a valve amp, muted and short, locked with the kick in 8ths\", cut in the same pass — a literal `mirror_kick`. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocity bands and every melody number; the research marks the specific tempo values as inference and [LOW-CONF] while sourcing the recording philosophy.",
